  annotation "note_1771574615263_0", anchor: "the sources gloss as \"the seizure\" without elaboration", text: "A masterclass in saying nothing with conviction. The E'frostlings — who have since lost Terrace Momiou to their own splinter faction and whose authority over anything is increasingly theoretical — seized a merchant guild's trade hub in a geothermally active corruption zone, and nobody wrote down why. Or everybody wrote it down and somebody sealed those records too. Given how the E'frostlings handle inconvenient documentation, I know which possibility entertains me more.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771574615263_1", anchor: "incentivized occupation of remote sites regardless of risk", text: "The Clever Ice Age's expansion doctrine in miniature: fund the charter, skip the survey, commit the population, discover the contamination, seal the chambers, keep trading. Dawn Naong was not a failure of the doctrine. It was the doctrine performing exactly as designed — which is the most damning thing I can say about the Clever Ice Age, and I say it often. Every remote settlement from that era reads identically in the ice. The ones that survived did so despite their founding, never because of it.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771574615263_2", anchor: "the ice here no longer holds impressions", text: "Dead ice. Whatever the lower vaults recorded during or after the seizure is not hidden — it is *dissolved*. There is no archive to recover. The corruption ate the record. I find it genuinely comic that I have spent my career reading ice-memory only to keep encountering sites where the ice has gone mute at precisely the moment something interesting happened.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771574615263_3", anchor: "\"The Donning Count,\" which tallies crystals vanishing", text: "See my annotation on Kelm's Dark Verses. The fledglings of the Berg are assembling a more rigorous commercial audit trail than the Aureate's sealed ledgers ever provided. Copper-fault, overnight clouding — these are specific, observable corruption signatures. The children are doing materials science. The guild is doing calligraphy.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
